Laminar scheduler: don't fault reaping a thread with uninitialized td_sched

Applies to: freebsd-src/usr/src/sys/kern/sched_laminar.c
(the kernel tree is vendored/gitignored here; this patch is the tracked
record of the change. Apply into ~/src/bsd/freebsd-src, copy to the build
VM's /usr/src, then `make buildkernel KERNCONF=LAMINAR-DEV &&
make installkernel KERNCONF=LAMINAR-DEV INSTKERNNAME=laminar`.)

PROBLEM
  On the ZFS-free tessera boot, the first process reap in the single-user
  shell panicked:
    panic: vm_fault failed ... error 1   (far = 0xffffffffffffffff)
    laminar_lane_thread_dtor+0x14 -> item_dtor -> uma_zfree_arg
      -> proc_reap -> kern_wait6 -> sys_wait4
  laminar_lane_thread_dtor reads `le = td_get_sched(td)->ts_lane` and, if
  non-NULL, dereferences `le->le_td`. ts_lane is normally either NULL or a
  pointer into a per-CPU tdq's (never-freed) lane array — sched_fork_thread
  bzeros td_sched for every forked/created thread, and thread0's storage is
  BSS-zero. But a thread destroyed BEFORE it was ever scheduled (an aborted
  creation whose td_sched was allocated from a fresh UMA slab but never
  passed through sched_fork_thread) reaches thread_dtor with an
  uninitialized td_sched, so ts_lane holds garbage (observed: (void *)-1).
  The `le != NULL` guard passes and `le->le_td` faults the reaper.

FIX
  1. Range-check ts_lane against the kernel VA window before the le_td
     deref (VM_{MIN,MAX}_KERNEL_ADDRESS): a garbage pointer such as -1 is
     outside it, so the reaper skips a thread that never owned a lane
     instead of faulting. Clear ts_lane after a real teardown.
  2. Initialize ts_lane / ts_adopted to NULL in sched_laminar_init
     (thread0) for completeness (BSS already zeros it, but be explicit).

VERIFIED
  Rebuilt LAMINAR-DEV, booted the self-contained tessera root: reaches the
  single-user shell and runs commands (echo, ls — real spawn+reap) with no
  panic. Dev VM (same kernel, ZFS root) is unaffected.

--- a/usr/src/sys/kern/sched_laminar.c
+++ b/usr/src/sys/kern/sched_laminar.c
@@ include block (after #include <machine/smp.h>)
 #include <machine/smp.h>
+
+#include <vm/vm.h>
+#include <vm/vm_param.h>	/* VM_{MIN,MAX}_KERNEL_ADDRESS — thread_dtor guard */

@@ laminar_lane_thread_dtor
 static void
 laminar_lane_thread_dtor(void *arg __unused, struct thread *td)
 {
 	struct laminar_lane_entity *le = td_get_sched(td)->ts_lane;
-
-	if (__predict_false(le != NULL) && le->le_td == td)
-		laminar_lane_teardown_common(le, false);
-	td_get_sched(td)->ts_adopted = NULL;	/* K-b: dying adopter */
+
+	/*
+	 * ts_lane is always either NULL or a pointer into a per-CPU tdq's
+	 * (never-freed) lane array, EXCEPT for a thread destroyed before it
+	 * was ever scheduled: an aborted creation whose td_sched was never
+	 * bzeroed by sched_fork_thread. Such a thread reaches thread_dtor
+	 * with an uninitialized td_sched, so ts_lane can be garbage
+	 * (observed: (void *)-1). It never owned a lane, so range-check the
+	 * pointer before the le_td deref and skip anything that can't be a
+	 * real entity, rather than faulting the reaper.
+	 */
+	if (__predict_false(le != NULL) &&
+	    (vm_offset_t)le >= VM_MIN_KERNEL_ADDRESS &&
+	    (vm_offset_t)le < VM_MAX_KERNEL_ADDRESS &&
+	    le->le_td == td) {
+		laminar_lane_teardown_common(le, false);
+		td_get_sched(td)->ts_lane = NULL;
+	}
+	td_get_sched(td)->ts_adopted = NULL;	/* K-b: dying adopter */
 }

@@ sched_laminar_init (thread0 td_sched)
 	ts0->ts_home_node_conf = 0;
 	ts0->ts_home_node = -1;
 	ts0->ts_mem_bw = false;
+	ts0->ts_lane = NULL;		/* no deadline lane / adoption yet */
+	ts0->ts_adopted = NULL;
 }
